      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right that this can be a source of bias in medical practice, and it's crucial to approach dermatological disorders like melanoma without inherent assumptions. While the term \"standard patient\" originates from historical data where white skin was overrepresented, it's not a consensus that it represents the definitive or \"normal\" presentation.\n\nDoctors are trained to recognize that variations exist and that melanoma can affect people of all skin types. If a lesion does not fit the classic description associated with lighter skin, it's important to evaluate it appropriately, as melanoma can indeed present variably across different skin tones. Recognizing the susceptibility to and signs of melanoma in darker skin and the presence of atypical nevi lets healthcare professionals offer equitable medical care to all patients.",
